    num:"02", title:"Managing up", grammar:"Causative structures (have / get)", month:1,
    reading:`<p>The most underrated skill in any organisation is not leadership — it is the ability to manage upward. Managing up means shaping how your manager thinks, acts, and prioritises, without ever giving the impression that you are the one steering.</p>
<p>I learned this early in my career from a mentor who had spent thirty years in corporate environments. "You need to have your ideas heard before the meeting even starts," she told me. "Get the key people briefed in advance. Have the data prepared so your manager can present it as their own instinct. Get people aligned quietly, one conversation at a time."</p>
<p>This sounds manipulative on the surface. It isn't. It's strategic communication. You are not deceiving anyone — you are doing the groundwork that transforms a good idea into an actionable decision. Organisations are slow to change. They have enormous inertia. If you want something to happen, you need to have the right people convinced before the formal discussion begins.</p>
<p>The best operators I know never get things done by pushing. They get things done by creating the conditions in which decisions seem inevitable. They have the right question asked in the right room. They get the right story told to the right person. And when the decision comes, it feels like consensus — because, in a sense, it is.</p>`,
